{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And Melchizedek, etc.</b> Our Sages said (Nedarim 32b) that he is Shem. And the reason the verse interrupted the matter of the king of Salem between the going out of the king of Sodom to make his matters known to Abraham, etc., is to tell the praise of the righteous — what a difference there is between them and the wicked. For the king of Sodom went out to meet Abraham to see his face empty-handed, even though he was obligated to receive Abraham's face with a gift, as is fitting for a king, and he, the wicked one, went out with empty hands. But Shem the righteous, without any obligation of generosity, took counsel and received his face with bread and wine." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along the way that they of blessed memory said (Yoma 28b) that Abraham our father fulfilled even the eruv of cooked foods, which are rabbinic enactments. Therefore the verse informed us that Abraham was a prince, great and honored in everyone's eyes, and when the king of Sodom went out to meet him, presumably he would receive him with a gift of bread for him and for his servants and men of his army. But since Abraham would abstain from their food — from the bread because of פת של גויים (bread of gentiles), and from the wine because of סתם יינם (unsealed wine of gentiles) — therefore the king of Sodom was clever and contrived that he would have the gift of food prepared through a trustworthy man, who is Shem, from the hand of Shem into the hand of Abraham. And the novelty the verse let us hear is that even bread and wine, which are only rabbinic prohibitions, Abraham was scrupulous about — all the more so other matters in which there is a suspicion of Torah prohibition." "<b>Further,</b> the verse intends to inform us of the reason from which it entered the mind of the king of Sodom to say to Abraham ״וְהָרְכוּשׁ קַח לָךְ״ and not ask for everything. Therefore it says <b>וַיֵּצֵא מֶלֶךְ סְדוֹם לִקְרָאתוֹ</b>, and after his going out, the king of Salem went out, etc., and Abraham gave him a tithe of everything. From this the king of Sodom perceived that this man is declaring that the possessions are his, for a person does not tithe from what is not his own. Therefore he asked him for the persons, and as for the possessions — ״I concede that you may take them,״ which is what he said: <b>קַח לָךְ</b>. And this declares that he still had a claim to retain everything. But in order not to enter into a dispute with him, he compromised and said: Give me the persons, and as for the possessions I will not go down to judgment with you, but rather I agree that you may take them. And see the reason for this in the exposition of the verse ״תֶּן לִי הַנֶּפֶשׁ״." "<b>And he was a priest, etc.</b> Meaning: he was a priest, but his seed is not priestly, for the priesthood was given to Abraham, as it is written (Psalms 110:4) ״אַתָּה כֹהֵן לְעוֹלָם״. And the verse says ״עַל דִּבְרָתִי מַלְכִּי צֶדֶק״ — for he blessed Abraham that he should be a priest to El Elyon, and with his goodwill he gave the matter to Abraham, as I shall explain." "And the verse's saying <b>לְאֵל עֶלְיוֹן</b> — because they had intermediate deities, as is known among the fools — he said that this one is a priest to El Elyon over all the deities that they had then. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Nedarim 32b) that because of the sin that he put the blessing of Abraham before the blessing of his Maker, the priesthood was taken from him. And according to their words of blessed memory, the verse's saying <b>וְהוּא כֹהֵן</b> is also to be interpreted as a restriction from that point onward, that the verse restricted it on account of his sin spoken of in the matter. And even though the restriction precedes the mention of the sin — what of it, for the Torah was written afterward."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:25.451014267Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}